<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agustín de Iturbide</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1783–1824</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Agustín I of Mexico, Emperor Agustín I, Agustín I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agustín de Iturbide was a Mexican royalist officer who changed sides in 1821 and, with the insurgent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a>, issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-iguala">Plan of Iguala</a>, which brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In May 1822 he was proclaimed Emperor Agustín I of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a>, but opposition from congress and the army forced him to abdicate in March 1823. Returning from exile in 1824, he was arrested and executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide.jpg" alt="Agustín de Iturbide" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Primitivo Miranda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agustin_I_of_Mexico.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
